#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d655fc-fecd-566e-a112-395d1edf56c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339813} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6421c-a0ec-5264-8c92-9b0a637dca86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339814} {:answer/id #uuid "60d076cb-e78c-5ba9-891b-6fbb24e6b378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339815} {:answer/id #uuid "03f084cc-0963-58a0-b760-90872dd423c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339816} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bb5fe0-c4b7-539a-b5e3-526ba94a91ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde6421c-a0ec-5264-8c92-9b0a637dca86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339814} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e21619-c39f-5b4c-a291-814dec44aada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "392b0319-d12c-58e9-a1f7-7742e3726555" :question/math-academy-id "q-125457" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Interpret circulation as the sum of four convergent improper line integrals over the open quadrant arcs, with independent one-sided limits at $(\\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\\pm1)$. The field is undefined at those four points, so the original ordinary circulation is not defined." :question/worked-solution "The original field is undefined at the four axis intersections, so an ordinary line integral over the whole circle is not defined. For the explicit improper interpretation, $r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ traverses the unit circle counterclockwise. On each open quadrant arc,\n\n$$\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx=\\frac{-\\sin t}{\\sin t}\\,dt=-dt,\\qquad-\\frac{1}{x}\\,dy=\\frac{-\\cos t}{\\cos t}\\,dt=-dt.$$\n\nThus the scalar pullback is $-2$. Each endpoint has the finite one-sided limit $-2$, so each open-arc integral converges absolutely and equals $-\\pi$. Their sum is $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{imp}}=-4\\pi$, the unchanged option a. No principal-value cancellation is used, and $F$ remains undefined at the excluded points."}]}
